            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Latin for a ditch, then the Latin for a rampart."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: arbitror, arbitrārī",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "arbitror, arbitrārī — \"to be of the opinion, to judge\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Arbitror eum vērum dīcere. I believe he is telling the truth."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Deponent: arbitror, arbitrārī, arbitrātus sum, passive in form and active in meaning. Like putō it takes the accusative and infinitive. English arbitrate comes from it."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "arbitror is built on arbiter \"an eyewitness, an umpire\" (Lewis & Short; EtymDB): to judge a thing as one who saw it. English arbiter, arbitrate and arbitrary come from the same family."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more word for telling, explaining and describing at B1."
