          "title": "You'll want to know: cēnseō, cēnsēre",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "cēnseō, cēnsēre — \"to give an opinion, to propose; to assess\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Ego cēnseō pācem meliōrem esse quam bellum. In my opinion peace is better than war."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "cēnseō, cēnsēre, cēnsuī, cēnsum. The verb of a senator stating his view in the senate, and of the officer who counted and valued the citizens. English census comes from it."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "cēnseō is inherited from Proto-Italic kensēō \"to assess, to judge\" (EtymDB), and has a Sanskrit cousin meaning \"to declare\". English census comes from it."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
